// Lens Flare, pass 2 of 4 - Field Kit
// Ghosts, the anamorphic streak and the starburst - the three elements that
// are anchored to the EMITTER rather than the frame centre, so they all live
// in one pass alongside the emitter position this pass reads from pass 1.
//
// GHOSTS, rebuilt on the model from Hullin, Hanika, Ajdin, Seidel, Kautz and
// Lensch, "Physically-Based Real-Time Lens Flare Rendering" (SIGGRAPH 2011):
// every ghost is light that partially reflects off ONE PAIR of lens surfaces
// before reaching the sensor, refracting normally at every other surface. An
// N-surface lens gives a finite, IRREGULAR set of C(N,2) such paths, each
// with its own axial position, size, brightness and dispersion - the opposite
// of evenly spaced mirrored copies, which is the strongest tell of a fake
// flare. Real-time cannot ray-trace that per pixel, so the paraxial result is
// precomputed offline into the per-`lens` tables below (ghostDataPrime /
// ghostDataZoom / ghostDataAnamorphic and their *DispB companions), read here
// as a fixed comparison chain exactly like lensDistort.sksl's per-profile
// tables - SkSL has no dynamic array indexing.
//
// WHERE THE PRIME AND ZOOM TABLES COME FROM. Real prescriptions - radius,
// thickness, index, Abbe number and AR-coating flag per surface - ported from
// "flaresim_nuke" by LocalStarlight (MIT licence), whose lens_files/ holds
// photonstophotos.net optical-bench data converted to plain text: a 13-surface
// Nikkor-S Auto 50mm f/1.4 for Fast Prime, a 28-surface AF-S Nikkor 24-70mm
// f/2.8G ED for Standard Zoom. For every pair of surfaces (k, l) this pass's
// build script paraxially traces two rays - a small-field chief ray and a
// stop-edge marginal ray - through the physical path light takes to form that
// ghost (refract 0..l, REFLECT at l, refract back to k, REFLECT at k, refract
// k..sensor, unfolding each reflection as a refraction into a medium of
// negated index, the standard trick for folded paraxial systems), at three
// wavelengths derived from each glass's own Abbe number. That gives, per
// ghost: an axial-position multiplier (the ratio of the ghost's image height
// to the direct image's, at the sensor), a size (the marginal ray's spread),
// an intensity (the product of the two reflecting surfaces' Fresnel
// reflectance and every other surface's transmittance, coated surfaces taken
// as roughly a tenth of a bare interface's reflectance - the .lens files flag
// coating but not its exact recipe), and a per-channel dispersion (how much
// the axial multiplier itself shifts with wavelength - tiny in real units,
// scaled up here by `ghostChromatic` because true chromatic ghost separation
// is imperceptible at flare scale). Only the eight strongest ghosts by that
// intensity survive into each table, sorted descending, which is what makes
// `ghostCount` reveal them in order of real importance rather than distance.
//
// THE ANAMORPHIC TABLE IS NOT MEASURED. No anamorphic prescription exists in
// flaresim_nuke's lens_files (it is a photographic-lens archive), so those
// eight rows are hand-derived from the same paper's model - fewer, more
// erratically spaced ghosts, several thrown well past frame centre - rather
// than fabricated to look like real optical-bench output. `lens` also carries
// a fixed horizontal squeeze for the anamorphic case, since a cylindrical
// front group elongates every ghost and the streak, not just the bokeh.
//
// CAT'S EYE. `ghostCatsEye` applies lensSegment.sksl's optical-vignetting
// treatment to every ghost individually: the aperture polygon clipped by a
// circle slid toward frame centre, radially outward from EACH GHOST's own
// position rather than the pixel being shaded, so ghosts that land off-axis
// (the common case once ghostSpacing throws them past the frame edge) get cut
// into lemons instead of staying perfect circles - the same barrel clipping
// that shapes real out-of-focus highlights.
//
// STARBURST is the far-field diffraction pattern of the aperture, not a drawn
// sunburst: spikes sit normal to each blade edge (an n-gon has n spikes when n
// is even, 2n when n is odd - opposite edges of an odd polygon are not
// parallel, so each contributes its own spike direction, a real fact about
// photographed starbursts), each with a sinc-squared cross-section rather
// than a smooth cosine lobe, and each channel's angular width scales with its
// wavelength - red diffracts wider than blue - so a spike is white near the
// emitter and spreads into spectral colour along its length as the physical
// separation between the per-channel lobes grows with radius. `blades` drives
// both this and the ghost iris, so a scene's starburst and its bokeh agree
// about the aperture, and there is no separate Points control left to
// disagree with it.
//
// STREAK. A soft anamorphic bar through the emitter, gated by how bright the
// emitter itself actually is - a manual position aimed at a dim area should
// not conjure a bar out of nothing. Kept as a creative control rather than a
// derived one; real anamorphic streak comes from the same cylindrical group
// as the squeeze above, but its length and colour are a stylistic choice this
// filter leaves to the user.
//
// Ported from flaresim_nuke by LocalStarlight (MIT) - see above for exactly
// which numbers. Loosely inspired by the ghost/halo/streak split in "Ultimate
// Lens Flare" by ektogamat (CC0) - see lensFlareCombine.sksl for that credit.

uniform float  colourSource;     // enum: 0 from the image, 1 the manual colour
uniform float  brightnessSource; // enum: 0 from the image, 1 constant
uniform half4  flareColour;  // used when colourSource is Manual
uniform float  threshold;
uniform float  gamma;

uniform float  lens;           // enum: 0 Fast Prime, 1 Standard Zoom, 2 Anamorphic
uniform float  ghostCount;
uniform float  ghostSpacing;
uniform float  ghostScale;
uniform float  ghostChromatic;
uniform float  ghostCatsEye;
uniform float  ghostCatsEyeFalloff;
uniform float  ghostIntensity;
uniform float  ghostSoft;      // 0 = hard polygon, 1 = fully defocused
uniform float  blades;        // 0 = round; shared with the starburst below

uniform float  streakLength;
uniform float  streakAngle;
uniform half4  streakTint;
uniform float  streakIntensity;

uniform float  starLength;
uniform float  starRotation;
uniform float  starSoftness;  // widens the spikes and damps their ringing
uniform float  starGlow;      // veiling glow around the core
uniform float  starIntensity;

uniform float2 resolution;
uniform float2 rectCentre;

uniform shader childShader;   // pass 1: the emitter-position buffer
uniform shader original;      // untouched input - MUST be declared last

const int   MAX_GHOSTS = 8;
const float PI = 3.14159265359;

// Same grade Glow uses: threshold up from black, then a gamma response.
float3 highlights(float2 coord) {
    half4 c = original.eval(coord);
    float3 lin = c.a > 0.0 ? float3(c.rgb / c.a) : float3(0.0);
    float bp = clamp(threshold, 0.0, 0.999);
    return pow(clamp((lin - bp) / max(1.0 - bp, 0.0001), 0.0, 1e4),
               float3(max(gamma, 0.0001)));
}

// Non-trig hash for the starburst's per-spike jitter - the argument here is
// always a small blade index, never a screen position, so this is really just
// a house-style default rather than a fix for the large-coordinate fract(sin)
// problem, kept consistent with filmDamage.sksl's building block.
float hash1(float n) {
    n = fract(n * 0.1031);
    n *= n + 33.33;
    n *= n + n;
    return fract(n);
}

// --- Ghost tables -----------------------------------------------------
// (axialMultiplier, relativeScale, relativeIntensity, dispersionR), plus a
// matching *DispB(i) for the blue channel - see the file header for how these
// four numbers were paraxially traced from each lens's real surfaces.

float4 ghostDataPrime(int i) {
    if (i == 0) { return float4( 0.3553, 0.2643, 1.0000,  0.0048); }
    if (i == 1) { return float4( 2.0579, 0.4285, 0.9980,  0.0033); }
    if (i == 2) { return float4( 0.0772, 0.5263, 0.9258,  0.0174); }
    if (i == 3) { return float4( 2.4002, 1.4362, 0.8216, -0.0094); }
    if (i == 4) { return float4( 1.7548, 0.3120, 0.8130,  0.0132); }
    if (i == 5) { return float4(-3.5581, 1.5000, 0.7341,  0.0182); }
    if (i == 6) { return float4( 1.3462, 0.8388, 0.6221, -0.0005); }
    return               float4(-1.4494, 0.2434, 0.5057, -0.0148);
}
float ghostDispBPrime(int i) {
    if (i == 0) { return -0.0048; }
    if (i == 1) { return -0.0032; }
    if (i == 2) { return -0.0172; }
    if (i == 3) { return  0.0095; }
    if (i == 4) { return -0.0131; }
    if (i == 5) { return -0.0180; }
    if (i == 6) { return  0.0007; }
    return               0.0148;
}

float4 ghostDataZoom(int i) {
    if (i == 0) { return float4(-2.4258, 0.5089, 1.0000, -0.0286); }
    if (i == 1) { return float4( 8.5849, 1.3255, 0.9585,  0.0720); }
    if (i == 2) { return float4(-4.7614, 1.0569, 0.8676,  0.0188); }
    if (i == 3) { return float4(-4.6891, 1.5000, 0.7196,  0.0005); }
    if (i == 4) { return float4( 2.1715, 0.6130, 0.7177, -0.0253); }
    if (i == 5) { return float4(-1.1471, 0.4173, 0.6996, -0.0292); }
    if (i == 6) { return float4( 5.4104, 0.7933, 0.6926,  0.0396); }
    return               float4( 1.7931, 0.3092, 0.6747,  0.0089);
}
float ghostDispBZoom(int i) {
    if (i == 0) { return  0.0278; }
    if (i == 1) { return -0.0685; }
    if (i == 2) { return -0.0211; }
    if (i == 3) { return -0.0018; }
    if (i == 4) { return  0.0266; }
    if (i == 5) { return  0.0281; }
    if (i == 6) { return -0.0376; }
    return               -0.0087;
}

// Hand-derived from the paper's model, NOT measured - see the file header.
float4 ghostDataAnamorphic(int i) {
    if (i == 0) { return float4( 0.42, 0.30, 1.00,  0.02); }
    if (i == 1) { return float4( 1.15, 0.55, 0.85, -0.03); }
    if (i == 2) { return float4(-0.65, 0.20, 0.60,  0.04); }
    if (i == 3) { return float4( 2.30, 0.90, 0.55,  0.01); }
    if (i == 4) { return float4(-1.80, 0.35, 0.40, -0.05); }
    if (i == 5) { return float4( 3.10, 1.20, 0.30,  0.06); }
    if (i == 6) { return float4( 0.85, 0.15, 0.20,  0.00); }
    return               float4(-2.60, 0.50, 0.15,  0.02);
}
float ghostDispBAnamorphic(int i) {
    if (i == 0) { return -0.05; }
    if (i == 1) { return  0.06; }
    if (i == 2) { return -0.02; }
    if (i == 3) { return  0.03; }
    if (i == 4) { return  0.02; }
    if (i == 5) { return -0.04; }
    if (i == 6) { return  0.01; }
    return               -0.01;
}

float4 ghostData(int lensSel, int i) {
    if (lensSel == 1) { return ghostDataZoom(i); }
    if (lensSel == 2) { return ghostDataAnamorphic(i); }
    return ghostDataPrime(i);
}
float ghostDispB(int lensSel, int i) {
    if (lensSel == 1) { return ghostDispBZoom(i); }
    if (lensSel == 2) { return ghostDispBAnamorphic(i); }
    return ghostDispBPrime(i);
}
// Anamorphic's cylindrical front group elongates every ghost horizontally.
float lensSqueezeX(int lensSel) { return lensSel == 2 ? 2.0 : 1.0; }

// A regular Blades-gon, as a soft radial mask, optically vignetted the way
// lensSegment.sksl clips its bokeh aperture: a circle the size of the
// aperture slid toward frame centre from `clipDir`, capped below a full
// aperture radius so a highlight turns into a lemon rather than vanishing.
// `squeezeX` widens the whole shape horizontally, for the anamorphic table.
// Returns the ghost's shape as (normalised radius, clip factor), so the caller
// can derive all three colour channels from ONE evaluation. It used to return a
// finished mask and be called three times per ghost for dispersion - 24 full
// evaluations across the loop, each with polygon maths, a pow() and three
// smoothsteps. That compiles as SkSL and then fails to BUILD, because the
// unrolled program overruns the backend's limit: "buildShader failed" with no
// compile error to point at. Splitting the expensive geometry from the cheap
// per-channel ramp keeps the same look at a third of the cost.
float2 irisField(float2 p, float radius, float bladeCount, float squeezeX,
                 float catsEyeShift, float catsEyeFalloffPow, float2 clipDir) {
    p.x /= max(squeezeX, 0.01);
    float d = length(p);
    float polyRadius;
    if (bladeCount < 3.0) {
        polyRadius = radius;
    } else {
        float ang = atan(p.y, p.x);
        float seg = 2.0 * PI / bladeCount;
        float wedge = ang - seg * floor(ang / seg + 0.5);
        polyRadius = radius * cos(seg * 0.5) / max(cos(wedge), 0.2);
    }

    float clipped = 1.0;
    if (catsEyeShift > 0.0) {
        float2 o = p / max(radius, 0.0001);
        float  r = clamp(length(o), 0.0, 1.0);
        float  shift = catsEyeShift * pow(r, max(catsEyeFalloffPow, 0.1));
        float  aa = mix(0.08, 0.45, clamp(ghostSoft, 0.0, 1.0));
        clipped = 1.0 - smoothstep(1.0, 1.0 + 2.0 * aa,
                                    length(o - clipDir * (shift * 0.7)));
    }
    return float2(d / max(polyRadius, 0.0001), clipped);
}

// The cheap half: turn a normalised radius into the ghost's falloff. A ghost is
// an image formed at the WRONG conjugate, so it is badly out of focus - soft
// edged, with the interior falling off rather than sitting flat.
float irisShape(float rn) {
    float soft = clamp(ghostSoft, 0.0, 1.0);
    float inner = mix(0.85, 0.02, soft);
    float poly = 1.0 - smoothstep(inner, 1.0, rn);
    // Subtle: a strong centre falloff would read as a ring rather than a disc.
    poly *= mix(1.0, 1.0 - 0.35 * clamp(rn, 0.0, 1.0), soft);
    return poly;
}

// sinc(x) = sin(pi*x) / (pi*x), the cross-section a real diffraction spike
// actually has - a thin central lobe plus faint side ripples, not a smooth
// drawn cosine lobe.
float sincSq(float x) {
    float px = PI * x;
    float s = abs(px) < 1e-4 ? 1.0 : sin(px) / px;
    return s * s;
}

// The flare's colour. From Image samples the source at the emitter. Manual
// keeps the source's BRIGHTNESS but substitutes the hue, so the flare still
// responds to how blown out the highlight is instead of sitting at a constant
// colour everywhere - which is what a flat tint would do.
float3 flareLight(float2 emitterPos) {
    float3 src = highlights(emitterPos);
    float  lum = dot(src, float3(0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722));

    // Colour and brightness are separate choices. Taking the hue from the image
    // divides its own luminance back out, so that when both are set to From
    // Image the result is exactly the sampled colour and nothing changes.
    float3 hue = colourSource < 0.5
        ? (lum > 0.0001 ? src / lum : float3(1.0))
        : float3(flareColour.rgb);

    // Manual brightness means the flare renders at full strength wherever it is
    // placed, instead of fading out because nothing bright happens to sit under
    // the emitter - which is the point of placing one by hand.
    float bright = brightnessSource < 0.5 ? lum : 1.0;
    return hue * bright;
}

half4 main(float2 fragCoord) {
    // Decode the emitter offset written by pass 0 - see that shader's note on
    // why it is stored biased into 0..1 rather than as raw pixels.
    float2 encE = float2(childShader.eval(fragCoord).rg);
    float2 emitterPos = rectCentre + (encE - 0.5) * length(max(resolution, float2(1.0)));
    float3 acc = float3(0.0);

    // GHOSTS - each one a copy of the highlight buffer, sized, placed and
    // dispersed by its own row in the table above, then optically vignetted.
    int lensSel = int(clamp(lens, 0.0, 2.0));
    float squeezeX = lensSqueezeX(lensSel);
    float gCount = clamp(floor(ghostCount), 0.0, float(MAX_GHOSTS));

    if (ghostIntensity > 0.0 && gCount > 0.0) {
        // The source's integrated colour. A ghost is the aperture uniformly
        // lit by an out-of-focus source, so every ghost carries this one
        // colour rather than a resampled picture of the scene.
        float3 ghostLight = flareLight(emitterPos);

        float2 axis = rectCentre - emitterPos;
        float  axisLen = length(axis);
        float2 axisDir = axisLen > 0.0001 ? axis / axisLen : float2(1.0, 0.0);

        for (int i = 0; i < MAX_GHOSTS; i++) {
            if (float(i) >= gCount) { continue; }

            float4 row = ghostData(lensSel, i);
            float mult      = row.x;
            float baseScale = row.y;
            float baseInten = row.z;
            float dispR     = row.w;
            float dispB     = ghostDispB(lensSel, i);

            float2 ghostCentre = rectCentre + axis * (ghostSpacing * mult);

            // Dispersion delta is a fraction of the axial multiplier - tiny in
            // real units, so it is scaled into visible pixels by how far the
            // ghost itself sits from the emitter, and by the user's control.
            float chromaR = dispR * ghostChromatic * 40.0 * axisLen;
            float chromaB = dispB * ghostChromatic * 40.0 * axisLen;

            // Cat's eye direction is the GHOST's own offset from frame centre,
            // not the pixel's - it is the ghost's field position that decides
            // how hard the barrel clips it, exactly like a real out-of-focus
            // highlight sitting off-axis.
            float2 res = max(resolution, float2(1.0));
            float2 fromCentre = (ghostCentre - rectCentre) / (0.5 * min(res.x, res.y));
            float  rC = length(fromCentre);
            float2 clipDir = rC > 1e-4 ? fromCentre / rC : float2(0.0);

            float irisRadius = max(res.x, res.y) * 0.12 * max(ghostScale, 0.01) * baseScale;
            // One geometry evaluation, three cheap ramps. Dispersion becomes a
            // RADIAL offset of the rim per channel, which is what a real
            // ghost's chromatic edge is anyway - the rim carries the colour.
            float2 fld = irisField(fragCoord - ghostCentre, irisRadius, blades, squeezeX,
                                    ghostCatsEye, ghostCatsEyeFalloff, clipDir);
            float dR = chromaR / max(irisRadius, 0.0001);
            float dB = chromaB / max(irisRadius, 0.0001);
            float3 m = float3(irisShape(fld.x - dR),
                              irisShape(fld.x),
                              irisShape(fld.x - dB)) * fld.y;

            acc += ghostLight * m * baseInten * ghostIntensity;
        }
    }

    // STREAK - a soft bar through the emitter, gated by its own brightness.
    if (streakIntensity > 0.0) {
        float3 emitterGlow = flareLight(emitterPos);
        float  ang = radians(streakAngle);
        float2 dir = float2(cos(ang), sin(ang));
        float2 toPixel = fragCoord - emitterPos;
        float  along = dot(toPixel, dir);
        float  perp  = length(toPixel - dir * along);
        float  width = max(streakLength * 0.015, 1.0);
        float  band  = exp(-(perp * perp) / (width * width));
        float  along_falloff = exp(-abs(along) / max(streakLength, 1.0));

        acc += float3(streakTint.rgb) * emitterGlow * band * along_falloff
             * streakIntensity;
    }

    // STARBURST - the aperture's own far-field diffraction pattern.
    if (starIntensity > 0.0) {
        float3 emitterGlow = flareLight(emitterPos);
        float2 toPixel = fragCoord - emitterPos;
        float  dist = length(toPixel);
        float  ang = atan(toPixel.y, toPixel.x) + radians(starRotation);

        float n = floor(max(blades, 0.0) + 0.5);
        float radial = exp(-dist / max(starLength, 1.0));

        // Core glow. The sharp centre IS correct - it is the diffraction
        // pattern's central lobe, which really is a hard bright point. But a
        // photograph never shows it bare: the same lens that diffracts the
        // light also scatters it, so the core always sits inside a veiling
        // glow. Without that, the spikes read as a drawn asterisk rather than
        // as a light source. The glow widens with softness for the same reason
        // the spikes do.
        float glowR = max(starLength, 1.0) * 0.18 * (0.5 + max(starSoftness, 0.0));
        float coreGlow = exp(-(dist * dist) / max(glowR * glowR, 1e-4));
        acc += emitterGlow * coreGlow * clamp(starGlow, 0.0, 1.0) * starIntensity;

        if (n < 3.0) {
            // Round aperture: no blade edges to diffract off, so the far
            // field is the Airy pattern's concentric rings instead of spikes.
            // A real Airy pattern is a bright core with FAINT rings: the
            // secondary maxima are about 1.75%, 0.42% and 0.16% of the peak.
            // cos^4 alone gave rings nearly as bright as the core, which is why
            // a round aperture read as a bullseye rather than as a point of
            // light. The 1/(1+r^3) envelope supplies that decay.
            float ringFreq = 2.0 * PI / max(starLength * 0.1, 1.0);
            float3 ratio = float3(1.19, 1.0, 0.877);   // red/green/blue wavelength ratios
            float3 ring = pow(abs(cos(dist * ringFreq * ratio)), float3(4.0));
            float  rings = dist * ringFreq / (2.0 * PI);          // ring ordinal
            float  decay = 1.0 / (1.0 + 40.0 * rings * rings * rings);
            float  core  = exp(-rings * rings * 6.0);
            acc += emitterGlow * (float3(core) + ring * decay) * radial * starIntensity;
        } else {
            // An n-gon has n diffraction spikes when n is even (opposite edges
            // are parallel and reinforce), 2n when n is odd (they are not) -
            // the same rule real photographed starbursts follow.
            float numSpikes = mod(n, 2.0) < 0.5 ? n : n * 2.0;
            float spikeStep = 2.0 * PI / max(numSpikes, 1.0);
            float idx = floor(ang / spikeStep + 0.5);
            float spikeAngle = idx * spikeStep;
            float delta = ang - spikeAngle;
            delta = atan(sin(delta), cos(delta));   // wrap to [-pi, pi]

            // Softness widens each spike and, above 1, damps the sinc's side
            // ripples toward a plain gaussian - a real spike is only as crisp
            // as the aperture edge that made it, and a perfectly sharp one
            // reads as drawn.
            float soft = max(starSoftness, 0.0);
            float halfWidth = spikeStep * 0.16 * (0.35 + soft);
            float3 ratio = float3(1.19, 1.0, 0.877);
            float3 spike = float3(sincSq(delta / (halfWidth * ratio.r)),
                                   sincSq(delta / (halfWidth * ratio.g)),
                                   sincSq(delta / (halfWidth * ratio.b)));
            if (soft > 1.0) {
                float g = exp(-(delta * delta) / max(halfWidth * halfWidth, 1e-6));
                spike = mix(spike, float3(g), clamp(soft - 1.0, 0.0, 1.0));
            }

            // A touch of per-blade irregularity - a real aperture's blades are
            // never machined perfectly identically, and identical spikes are
            // as strong a "this is drawn" tell as even ghost spacing.
            float jitter = mix(0.7, 1.3, hash1(idx * 7.31));

            acc += emitterGlow * spike * radial * jitter * starIntensity;
        }
    }

    return half4(half3(clamp(acc, 0.0, 64.0)), half(1.0));
}
